WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.027
CI/CD turns patching into a repeatable flow.

00:00:04.027 --> 00:00:13.265
Dependency-update tools can propose new base images or library versions, but proposals still need ownership, review, and testing.

00:00:13.265 --> 00:00:20.608
Unreviewed automation can introduce incompatible code, untrusted artifacts, or changes that bypass risk decisions.

00:00:20.608 --> 00:00:26.056
Pipeline scans evaluate source dependencies, build inputs, and completed images.

00:00:26.056 --> 00:00:37.189
Policy gates enforce defined requirements before promotion, such as approved base images, acceptable vulnerability posture, successful tests, required SBOMs, or reviewed exceptions.

00:00:37.189 --> 00:00:41.689
Build provenance records how an artifact was created.

00:00:41.689 --> 00:00:52.822
Signing associates an approved identity with the image digest, while verification helps registries and clusters reject artifacts that do not meet policy.

00:00:52.822 --> 00:00:58.744
Protect signing keys, build identities, and pipeline credentials as high-value assets.

00:00:58.744 --> 00:01:04.666
Registry controls limit who can push, retag, delete, and pull images.

00:01:04.666 --> 00:01:10.114
Retain immutable digests, scanning results, SBOMs, signatures, and lifecycle records.

00:01:10.114 --> 00:01:18.878
Promotion should move the same tested artifact through environments instead of rebuilding unrelated copies for each stage.

00:01:18.878 --> 00:01:23.379
Automation should surface decisions rather than hide them.

00:01:23.379 --> 00:01:31.195
Clear reports explain the changed dependency, risk addressed, tests run, policy result, approver, and destination.

00:01:31.195 --> 00:01:37.117
Exceptions should be explicit, time-limited, and visible to later promotion steps.

00:01:37.117 --> 00:01:41.144
Measure pipeline reliability as well as speed.

00:01:41.144 --> 00:01:50.856
Failed updates, stale pull requests, bypassed gates, unsigned images, and differences between approved and deployed digests reveal process weakness.

00:01:50.856 --> 00:01:57.725
Good automation shortens remediation while preserving evidence, separation of duties, and controlled release.
